From 243fd4a544f8c5459d13d33afe426dbede75f3f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Duc Anh Luu <luuducanh05051995@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 23:16:36 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] Retransmit the DTLS 1.3 ClientHello when sending early data

When a DTLS 1.3 client resumes a session and sends early data, it
switches to the early data write key right after sending its
ClientHello. tls13_change_cipher_state() treated that key change like
the start of a new flight and cleared the sent message buffer. At that
point the version has not been negotiated yet, so the logic that keeps
unacknowledged DTLS 1.3 messages did not apply and the ClientHello was
dropped from the retransmit queue. With the queue empty,
ssl_set_new_record_layer() then also freed the epoch 0 write record
layer that a retransmitted ClientHello would need.

As a result the retransmit timer kept running but had nothing to send:
if the first flight was lost, the client never resent its ClientHello
and the handshake stalled. Without early data the problem did not occur.

The early data is part of the same flight as the ClientHello, which the
server has not answered yet, so do not clear the sent buffer when the
early data write key is installed. Since the ClientHello can now outlive
the switch to the early data write record layer, free the record layer
it was sent with once the buffered ClientHello is released, in the same
way as is already done for the CCS, Finished, ServerHello and KeyUpdate
messages.

ssl_set_new_record_layer() kept the old DTLS write record layer
whenever there were buffered sent messages. With the ClientHello now
staying buffered, the early data record layer can be replaced while
the queue still holds the ClientHello, for example when the server
answers with a HelloRetryRequest, and no buffered message was sent with
that record layer, so nothing freed it. Only keep the old write record
layer if a buffered message was sent with it.

The buffered ClientHello now also frees its write record layer when it
is released, so it must not be left pointing at a layer that has
already been freed. SSL_set_connect_state() and SSL_set_accept_state()
call RECORD_LAYER_reset(), which freed the current write record layer
while a buffered ClientHello still referenced it: SSL_free() then freed
it a second time, and a retransmission timeout used it. Drop the
buffered messages in RECORD_LAYER_reset() before freeing the record
layers, as they belong to the handshake being abandoned.

Add a test that drops the client's first flight in a resumed DTLS 1.3
handshake with early data and checks that the ClientHello is
retransmitted and the handshake completes, and a test that calls
SSL_set_connect_state() while a DTLS 1.2 or DTLS 1.3 ClientHello is
buffered.

Fixes #33127

Reviewed-by: Mounir Idrassi <mounir.idrassi@idrix.fr>
Reviewed-by: Matt Caswell <matt@openssl.foundation>
Merge-date: Tue Nov 10 14:24:43 2026
Merged-from: https://github.com/openssl/openssl/pull/33128
(cherry picked from commit 14a6de1652f6df9809ef4b888a7c7769a8c533cf)
